  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "Stellar Playing‑in‑the‑Band sandwich with soaring solos",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers a monumental jam sandwich that weaves Mississippi Half‑Step, Playing In The Band, El Paso, Wharf Rat and Morning Dew into one seamless journey. Jerry Garcia’s guitar soars on Jack Straw, Big River and the extended jams, while Keith Godchaux adds jazzy depth throughout. Overall a tight, high‑energy performance captured in excellent soundboard quality.",
    "review": "On November 21, 1973 at the Denver Coliseum the Dead rolled out one of their most memorable second‑set marathons. Kicking off with Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo, they launched into an epic Playing In The Band sandwich that threaded El Paso, a second burst of Playing In The Band, Wharf Rat and yet another return to the theme before soaring into a heartfelt Morning Dew. The band’s chemistry is palpable – Jerry Garcia’s tone is flawless, delivering flute‑like feedback on the jam and lyrical solos on Jack Straw and Big River, while Phil Lesh lays down bottom‑end melodies that weave through the improvisations. Keith Godchaux’s keyboards sparkle with crisp breaks and jazzy comping, giving the jams both structure and swing. Bob Weir provides solid rhythm work and tasteful phasing on Wharf Rat, and Bill Kreutzmann drives the groove with a snap‑tight feel throughout. The first set shines as well, highlighted by Here Comes Sunshine, Weather Report Suite and Brokedown Palace, but it’s the second‑set marathon that cements this show as a late‑’73 classic. Captured on Charlie Miller’s pristine soundboard transfer, the mix is crystal clear with balanced guitars, keyboards and vocals, making this recording the definitive version of a truly legendary performance.",
